-- title:
--   Uniformiser in K₁ at non-cuspidal places of X₁(Mp)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $M$ be a nonzero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, and let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$. Inside the field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ of formal Laurent series over $L$, consider two intermediate fields over $L$: $K$, assumed equal to `laurentBaseChange L (x1FunctionField (M * p))`, i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ of the $\mathbb{Q}$-field generated by the $q$-expansions in `intFormRatiosC ℚ (Gamma1 (M*p))`; and $K_1$, assumed equal to the analogous $L$-subfield generated by the image of the $\mathbb{Q}$-field attached to $\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p)$. Let $J \in K$ be an element whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of `jq`, the $q$-expansion $q^{-1}\cdot(\text{numerator power series of } j)$ of the modular invariant. Let $x$ be a place of $K$ over $L$, that is, a valuation subring of $K$ containing $L$, not equal to $K$ and a principal ideal ring, with associated normalised valuation $\operatorname{ord}_x = -\log$ of its adic valuation; assume $\operatorname{ord}_x J \ge 0$. Then there exists $t \in K$ whose underlying Laurent series lies in $K_1$ and with $\operatorname{ord}_x t = 1$.
--
--   At a place of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ where the modular invariant is integral — a non-cuspidal place — the covering $X_1(Mp) \to X(\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p))$ is unramified, expressed here concretely as the existence of a uniformiser at $x$ already lying in the smaller function field. It is used in the analysis of the two-chart integral model of $X_1(M) \times X_1(M)/X_0(p)$-type curves, to show that the relevant chart is unramified away from the maximal points in question.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_ord_eq_one_of_place_x1FunctionField_mul_of_ord_nonneg.lean

